CAT = "rmaLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Before a mole removal, the provider explains the risks, benefits and other options, and the patient agrees in writing. What is this called?",
 "Informed consent", ["Implied consent", "An express contract", "An advance directive"],
 "Informed consent means the patient agrees after the provider explains the procedure, risks, benefits and alternatives. Implied consent is inferred from conduct or an emergency."),
("Which HIPAA rule sets administrative, physical and technical safeguards for electronic health information?",
 "The Security Rule", ["The Privacy Rule", "The Transactions Rule", "The Enforcement Rule"],
 "The Security Rule covers electronic PHI and requires safeguards such as access controls and audit logs. The Privacy Rule covers PHI in every form and who may see it."),
("Under HIPAA, for which purpose may PHI be shared without the patient's written authorization?",
 "Treatment or payment", ["Marketing a new service", "An employer's hiring choice", "A local news story"],
 "The Privacy Rule lets a practice use and share PHI for treatment, payment and health care operations without a signed authorization. Marketing and most other uses need one."),
("Which HIPAA document tells patients how the practice may use and share their health information?",
 "Notice of Privacy Practices", ["Release of information form", "Advance beneficiary notice", "Patient bill of rights"],
 "The Privacy Rule requires the practice to give patients its Notice of Privacy Practices and make a good-faith effort to get a signed acknowledgment."),
("What sets the limits on which clinical tasks a medical assistant may perform?",
 "State laws and regulations", ["The patient's insurer", "The American Medical Association", "The patient's family"],
 "A medical assistant's scope of practice is set by state law and regulations, which vary by state, and works under the delegation of a licensed provider."),
("What is the legal term for failing to act as a reasonably careful person would in the same situation?",
 "Negligence", ["Defamation", "Battery", "Fraud"],
 "Negligence is an unintentional failure to use reasonable care that causes harm. Battery is an intentional unconsented touching."),
("Which right is commonly listed in a patient bill of rights?",
 "To refuse treatment", ["To set their own fees", "To choose staff schedules", "To see others' records"],
 "Patient bills of rights include the right to respectful care, privacy, information about care and the right to refuse treatment."),
("Which statement about advance directives is true?",
 "They can be changed at any time", ["Only a lawyer may write one", "They expire after one year", "Family may freely override them"],
 "A competent adult may revoke or update an advance directive whenever they wish. A copy of the current version should be kept in the chart."),
("Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, who may be held liable for a medical assistant's acts on the job?",
 "The employer", ["The patient", "The insurer", "The state"],
 "Respondeat superior (let the master answer) makes an employer responsible for employees' acts within the scope of their work. Employees can still be liable themselves."),
("How does licensure differ from certification for health care workers?",
 "Licensure is granted by a state", ["Certification is always required by law", "Licensure is never renewed", "Certification is issued by the FDA"],
 "A license is a legal permission granted by a state government. Certification is granted by a professional organization after the person meets its standards, often by passing an exam."),
("Which principle of medical ethics means avoiding harm to the patient?",
 "Nonmaleficence", ["Beneficence", "Autonomy", "Confidentiality"],
 "Nonmaleficence is the duty to do no harm. Beneficence is the duty to act for the patient's good, and autonomy is respect for the patient's own choices."),
("A coworker asks the medical assistant to sign a chart note for a procedure the coworker did. What should she do?",
 "Decline to sign it", ["Sign it to help out", "Sign with both names", "Sign it in pencil"],
 "Each person documents and signs only the care they gave. Signing for someone else falsifies the record."),
]
